theory.intro:
A **reciprocal model** $y=\frac{k}{x}$ describes inverse variation. For positive quantities with $k>0$, as one quantity grows, the other shrinks. When $k\ne0$, its graph is a **hyperbola**. The constant $k$ is the product $xy$, which is the same for every pair of values.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.4,0) -- (6.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,domain=0.9:5.9,samples=100,variable=\x,<->] plot ({\x},{3.6/\x});
\node[right] at (2.3,2.5) {$y=\frac{k}{x}$};
\node[below] at (3.6,-0.2) {never touches the axes};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
The graph shows the average speed $s$ km/h needed to complete a $300$ km journey in $t$ hours.
(a) Use the graph to find the speed needed for a journey lasting $5$ hours.
(b) Use the graph to find the time taken at $100$ km/h.
(c) Explain why the graph never touches either axis and why only its positive branch applies.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{modelBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{gridGrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=left,axis line style={black,-{Stealth}},xmin=0,xmax=10,ymin=0,ymax=160,xtick={0,1,...,10},ytick={0,20,...,160},grid=major,grid style={gridGrey},scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$t$ (hours)},ylabel={$s$ (km/h)}]
\addplot[modelBlue,thick,<->,domain=1.95:9.8,samples=120] {300/x};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) Read up from $t=5$ to the curve, then across to the speed axis: $s=60$. The required speed is $60$ km/h.
(b) Read across from $s=100$ to the curve, then down to the time axis: $t=3$. The journey takes $3$ hours.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{modelBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{guideRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridGrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=left,axis line style={black,-{Stealth}},xmin=0,xmax=10,ymin=0,ymax=160,xtick={0,1,...,10},ytick={0,20,...,160},grid=major,grid style={gridGrey},scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$t$ (hours)},ylabel={$s$ (km/h)}]
\addplot[modelBlue,thick,<->,domain=1.95:9.8,samples=120] {300/x};
\draw[guideRed,dashed] (axis cs:5,0)--(axis cs:5,60)--(axis cs:0,60);
\draw[guideRed,dashed] (axis cs:0,100)--(axis cs:3,100)--(axis cs:3,0);
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,guideRed] coordinates {(5,60) (3,100)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(c) At $t=0$, $s=\frac{300}{0}$ is undefined: the journey cannot be completed in zero time. At $s=0$, the car does not travel, so it cannot complete the journey in any finite time. The axes are asymptotes. Time and speed must both be positive, so the negative branch has no meaning here.

workedExamples.question_text:
The graph shows the relationship between base length $b$ cm and perpendicular height $h$ cm for parallelograms of a fixed area.
(a) Read a point from the graph and use it to find $k$ in $h=\frac{k}{b}$.
(b) Write the model and explain what $k$ represents.
(c) Use the model to find the height when the base is $15$ cm.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{modelBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{gridGrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=left,axis line style={black,-{Stealth}},xmin=0,xmax=12,ymin=0,ymax=12,xtick={0,1,...,12},ytick={0,1,...,12},grid=major,grid style={gridGrey},scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$b$ (cm)},ylabel={$h$ (cm)}]
\addplot[modelBlue,thick,<->,domain=2.6:11.8,samples=120] {30/x};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) The curve passes through $(5,6)$.
$\begin{align*}k&=bh\\k&=5\times6\\k&=30\end{align*}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{modelBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{guideRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridGrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=left,axis line style={black,-{Stealth}},xmin=0,xmax=12,ymin=0,ymax=12,xtick={0,1,...,12},ytick={0,1,...,12},grid=major,grid style={gridGrey},scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$b$ (cm)},ylabel={$h$ (cm)}]
\addplot[modelBlue,thick,<->,domain=2.6:11.8,samples=120] {30/x};
\draw[guideRed,dashed] (axis cs:5,0)--(axis cs:5,6)--(axis cs:0,6);
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,guideRed] coordinates {(5,6)};
\path (axis cs:5,6) node[above right,text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$(5,6)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(b) The model is $h=\frac{30}{b}$. Since base times perpendicular height gives area, $bh=30$ means the fixed area is $30$ cm$^2$.
(c) Substitute $b=15$.
$\begin{align*}h&=\frac{30}{15}\\h&=2\text{ cm}\end{align*}$
A larger base gives a smaller height, as expected.

